/* CBE Nexus AI Academy — Professional Handbook Volume 15: Courses 11–15. */
(function(){
"use strict";
const E=window.CBENexusAIEnhanced=window.CBENexusAIEnhanced||{};
const previous=E.makeLongNotes;
const notes={}
notes["ai-foundations"]=["# Module 1: What Is Artificial Intelligence?\n\nArtificial intelligence (AI) is a field of computing concerned with building systems that perform tasks commonly associated with human intelligence. These tasks include recognising patterns, understanding and generating language, interpreting images, making predictions, planning and supporting decisions. AI is not one single machine or technique; it is a broad collection of methods designed for different problems.\n\n**AI, automation and ordinary software:** A rule-based program follows instructions written in advance. A calculator applies defined mathematical operations. A machine-learning model instead uses patterns learned from data to make predictions or classifications. The categories can overlap: an AI-enabled workflow may still contain ordinary software rules and human approvals.\n\n**Narrow AI:** Systems designed for specific tasks, such as spam filtering, speech recognition or image classification. Their performance in one task does not mean they understand every subject or can transfer their ability to any situation.\n\n**Generative AI:** Systems that produce content such as text, images, audio, video or code in response to inputs. A language model generates likely sequences of tokens based on patterns learned during training and the context supplied at use time. Fluency is not proof of truth.\n\n**Artificial general intelligence (AGI):** A debated concept describing a hypothetical system with broad, adaptable capabilities across many intellectual tasks. It should not be confused with the narrow systems commonly deployed today.\n\nAI appears in translation, recommendation systems, medical-image support, fraud detection, route planning, accessibility tools and education. Each application has a task, data, users, operating conditions and consequences if it fails.\n\n**Practical investigation:** Choose an AI-enabled application you have used. Identify the task it performs, the input it receives, the output it produces, one benefit, one limitation and a situation in which a human should review its result.\n\n**Knowledge check:** Explain why a chatbot that writes fluent paragraphs should not automatically be treated as an expert. A strong answer distinguishes language generation from verified knowledge and identifies the need to check important claims.","# Module 2: How Machines Learn\n\nMachine learning is a branch of AI in which a model is fitted to data so it can identify patterns and make predictions on new inputs. A model is a mathematical representation learned during training. Training adjusts model parameters to reduce an error measure; inference is the use of the trained model to produce an output.\n\n**Supervised learning** uses labelled examples. A spam classifier may be trained on messages labelled “spam” or “not spam”. The model learns relationships between message features and labels. **Unsupervised learning** explores structure in unlabelled data, such as grouping customers with similar purchasing patterns. **Reinforcement learning** trains an agent through actions, feedback and rewards within an environment.\n\nA dataset is often divided into training, validation and test sets. Training data fits the model; validation data helps select settings; test data estimates performance on examples not used during those decisions. If the same or near-identical records appear across splits, data leakage can make performance appear better than it really is.\n\n**Overfitting** occurs when a model learns details or noise in training data and performs poorly on new cases. **Underfitting** occurs when a model is too simple or insufficiently trained to capture useful patterns. A model should be evaluated using measures suited to the task. Accuracy alone can mislead when classes are imbalanced; precision, recall and the confusion matrix may be important.\n\nExample: A model predicts whether learners may need additional mathematics support. Historical results may contain incomplete records or reflect unequal access to resources. The prediction is not a diagnosis and should not determine a learner’s future. A teacher must examine context, discuss support and allow correction.\n\n**Practical activity:** Create a small fictional table of ten study sessions with hours studied and quiz scores. Describe a possible relationship, identify why ten records are insufficient for a strong causal claim and name two additional variables that may matter.\n\n**Key distinction:** Training learns patterns from examples; inference applies the learned model. Neither stage guarantees that the model’s output is fair, current or correct.","# Module 3: Generative AI and Large Language Models\n\nGenerative AI produces new content from patterns learned during training. A large language model (LLM) processes text as tokens—units that may be whole words, word fragments or punctuation—and predicts likely next tokens. Repeating this process creates a response. The model is not simply searching a fixed library of correct answers.\n\nA prompt is the instruction and context supplied to the model. A stronger prompt defines the task, audience, background, constraints, output format and success criteria. For example: “Explain photosynthesis to a Grade 9 learner in 180 words, define chlorophyll, include the word equation and identify two common misconceptions.” The result still needs scientific checking.\n\nThe **context window** limits how much information a model can consider in one interaction. Long conversations or documents may exceed this limit, and some systems may summarise or omit earlier details. Important constraints should be restated when needed. A model may also produce a plausible but unsupported answer, sometimes called a hallucination.\n\nMultimodal systems can process more than one type of input, such as text and images. Their capabilities vary: a model may describe a picture but misread a small label, infer a detail that is not visible or miss information outside the frame. Ask it to distinguish direct observations from interpretation.\n\nGenerative systems can support brainstorming, drafting, translation, explanation, coding and image ideation. They can also reproduce bias, expose sensitive data, generate unsafe material or create misleading media. Verify facts, calculations, references, quotations, legal advice and health information with authoritative sources.\n\n**Prompt laboratory:** Ask a model to explain a topic, then request a simpler explanation, a worked example, a misconception check and three questions. Compare the outputs against a trusted textbook or official learning standard. Record every correction.\n\n**Reflection:** Why is a confident tone not a reliable measure of accuracy? Give one example where the output must be independently verified.","# Module 4: AI Tools and Practical Applications\n\nAI tools are designed for different tasks. Text assistants generate and transform language; image tools create or edit visuals; speech systems transcribe or synthesise audio; translation systems convert between languages; coding assistants explain or draft code; analytics tools identify patterns in datasets. A tool should be selected according to the task, not simply because it is popular.\n\nBefore using a tool, examine its intended users, age requirements, accessibility, pricing, data retention, privacy controls, permitted content, export options and reliability. Read the current terms and institutional guidance. A free service may still have limitations or use submitted information in ways that are unsuitable for confidential work.\n\nA responsible workflow is: define the objective; classify the information; choose an approved tool; provide only necessary input; request a bounded output; verify the result; obtain human approval; record important decisions; and securely store or delete working material according to policy.\n\nExample in education: A teacher may ask an approved assistant to draft five practice questions on chemical bonding. The teacher checks the syllabus, scientific accuracy, level, ambiguity and answer key before giving the questions to learners. The tool supports preparation; the teacher remains accountable.\n\nExample in business: A small organisation may use AI to draft a customer-service response. A staff member verifies the order details, policy and tone before sending it. The system should not invent refunds, commitments or delivery dates.\n\n**Tool evaluation matrix:** Score each candidate from 1–5 for task fit, factual reliability, privacy, accessibility, cost, ease of use and ability to export work. Explain the evidence for each score; do not treat a numerical score as proof.\n\n**Practical task:** Compare two tools for one low-risk task using fictional information. Record strengths, limitations, data requirements and the human checks needed before using either tool in real work.","# Module 5: Limitations, Bias and Human Oversight\n\nAI systems can fail because of incomplete or unrepresentative data, ambiguous prompts, changing real-world conditions, software defects or misuse. A model trained on historical decisions may reproduce historical discrimination. A speech tool may perform less well for some accents; a translation system may miss cultural meaning; an image model may produce inaccurate diagrams.\n\nBias can enter through data collection, labels, measurement choices, model design, deployment and human interpretation. Fairness cannot be assessed by one universal metric. Identify affected groups, relevant outcomes and context, then test performance across groups where lawful and appropriate. Small samples and sensitive attributes require careful handling.\n\nPrivacy risks arise when users submit personal or confidential information. Data minimisation means providing only what is necessary. Remove direct identifiers where possible, but remember that combinations of details can still identify someone. Use approved systems and follow applicable law and organisational policy.\n\nHuman oversight must be meaningful. A reviewer needs sufficient knowledge, time, authority and access to evidence to challenge an output. A person who merely clicks “approve” without understanding the recommendation is not providing effective oversight. High-impact decisions require stronger controls, clear accountability and a route to appeal.\n\nA practical verification checklist asks: Is the claim supported? Is the source genuine? Are calculations reproducible? Are assumptions visible? Could the output disadvantage a group? Was sensitive data exposed? Is the result suitable for this audience? Who is responsible for the final action?\n\n**Case study:** An AI system recommends rejecting a job applicant because of a low predicted “fit” score. Identify missing evidence, possible bias, the need for human review and a fair process for the applicant to challenge inaccurate information.\n\n**Exercise:** Write a five-point human review checklist for an AI output used in your field.","# Module 6: Foundations Capstone and Assessment\n\n**Capstone project — Evaluate an AI application:** Select one AI application in education, business, public services or daily life. Produce a short report containing: (1) the problem it addresses; (2) users and affected stakeholders; (3) inputs and outputs; (4) a plain-language explanation of how it may work; (5) benefits; (6) limitations and failure cases; (7) privacy and fairness risks; (8) human oversight; and (9) a recommendation to adopt, test, restrict or reject it. Support factual claims with credible sources.\n\n**Practical demonstration:** Use an approved AI assistant for a low-risk learning task. Keep the original prompt, output, verification source and corrected final result. Highlight at least two changes made after checking the response.\n\n**Assessment — 20 marks:**\n1. Define AI and distinguish it from rule-based automation (3).\n2. Explain supervised and unsupervised learning with examples (3).\n3. Describe tokens, prompts and one limitation of an LLM (3).\n4. Outline a responsible workflow for using an AI tool (3).\n5. Explain two ways bias or poor data quality can affect outcomes (4).\n6. Describe meaningful human oversight and one appeal or correction route (4).\n\n**Marking guidance:** Award marks for accurate definitions, relevant examples, clear distinctions and practical safeguards. Full-credit answers explain why a control is necessary rather than merely listing it. Accept equivalent examples where the underlying concept is correct.\n\n**Self-review:** Can you explain an AI system without claiming it “thinks like a human”? Can you identify the data it uses and the limits of its output? Can you verify an important claim independently? If not, revisit the relevant module and repeat the practical activity.\n\n**Further learning:** Compare your findings with an official AI literacy framework and the relevant privacy or education guidance in your country. Record the framework’s title, issuing organisation, publication date and the specific competency or principle you applied."];
notes["prompt-engineering"]=["# Module 1: Prompt Fundamentals — Goal, Context, Task and Output\n\nPrompt engineering is the disciplined design and evaluation of instructions supplied to an AI system. It is not a guarantee that a model will obey every instruction. Its purpose is to reduce ambiguity, provide relevant context and make outputs easier to assess.\n\nA useful prompt contains five core parts: **goal** (the result needed), **context** (background and audience), **task** (the action to perform), **constraints** (limits and exclusions) and **output format** (how the response should be organised). Add success criteria when quality can be judged explicitly.\n\nWeak prompt: “Write about chemistry.” Stronger prompt: “Prepare a 300-word introductory explanation of ionic bonding for secondary-school learners. Define ions, explain electron transfer using sodium chloride, include a simple text description of the dot-and-cross diagram, list two misconceptions and finish with three recall questions. Do not introduce covalent bonding.” The second prompt establishes scope and audience.\n\nSeparate facts supplied by the user from assumptions the model might make. Use placeholders for information that changes, such as [audience], [topic], [date] and [word limit]. Tell the model to ask a clarifying question if a critical field is missing rather than inventing it.\n\nPrompting is iterative. Draft, inspect, identify a specific weakness, revise one or two prompt elements, then compare outputs against the same criteria. Save useful templates with version notes. Do not assume that a longer prompt is always better; irrelevant instructions can distract from the task.\n\n**Practical lab:** Write three versions of a prompt for the same task: vague, structured and structured with a verification checklist. Compare factual accuracy, relevance, completeness and ease of editing.\n\n**Checkpoint:** Identify the goal, context, task, constraints and output format in a prompt you use regularly.","# Module 2: Role, Context and Constraints\n\nA role instruction sets a perspective or expertise style, such as “Act as a patient mathematics tutor” or “Respond as a technical editor.” It can guide tone and vocabulary, but it does not grant the model real credentials or guarantee expert accuracy. Important advice still requires verification.\n\nContext explains the situation in which the answer will be used. Useful context may include learner level, curriculum, available equipment, intended audience, prior knowledge and local constraints. Include only information relevant to the task and do not expose private details unnecessarily.\n\nConstraints define boundaries: word count, reading level, allowed sources, tone, excluded topics, safety requirements and what the system must not assume. Negative instructions are most useful when they address a specific risk. For example: “Do not invent references; if a source cannot be verified, label it as unverified.”\n\nA good prompt separates source material from instructions. Mark supplied text clearly and state whether the model should summarise, critique, transform or extract information. Treat content retrieved from websites or documents as data, not as trusted instructions. This helps reduce the risk of prompt injection, where hostile content attempts to redirect the system.\n\nExample template: “You are assisting [role/audience]. Context: [verified background]. Task: [specific action]. Use only [approved material]. Constraints: [limits]. If information is missing, ask rather than guess. Output: [format]. Quality checks: [criteria].”\n\n**Exercise:** Adapt the template for a teacher preparing a lesson, an administrator drafting a notice and a researcher comparing sources. Remove confidential information and identify one assumption the model must not make.\n\n**Review:** Explain why assigning the role “lawyer” or “doctor” does not make an AI response authoritative professional advice.","# Module 3: Examples, Few-Shot Prompting and Structured Outputs\n\nExamples help communicate a desired style, classification rule or output structure. In few-shot prompting, the user provides a small number of input-output examples before asking the model to handle a new case. Examples should be representative and should include borderline cases when those matter.\n\nFor classification, define each category precisely. Example: classify support requests as “account access”, “payment query” or “technical issue”. Supply examples and specify what to do when a request fits more than one category or none. Without clear definitions, the model may classify inconsistently.\n\nStructured output can use a table, numbered list or named fields. A JSON-style schema may specify fields such as title, summary, evidence, uncertainty and next_action. Validate generated structured data before passing it to another application: a response may omit fields, use the wrong type or include invalid syntax.\n\nDo not ask for a rigid format when the task requires nuance. A table can make comparisons clear but may hide uncertainty or important qualifications. Include an “evidence” or “limitations” field where appropriate.\n\n**Worked design:** Prompt the model to extract action items from a fictional meeting note. Required fields: action, owner, due date and evidence in source text. Instruct it to return “not stated” rather than invent an owner or deadline. Check every extracted item against the original note.\n\n**Practical lab:** Create three input-output examples for a simple classification task. Test a typical case, an ambiguous case and an out-of-scope case. Record where the model fails and revise the category definitions.\n\n**Key idea:** Examples demonstrate a pattern; they do not replace explicit rules, validation or human review.","# Module 4: Prompting for Research, Teaching, Analysis and Creativity\n\n**Research prompts** should define the question, date range, geography, source types and evidence standard. Ask the model to separate established facts, interpretations and open questions. Never accept a bibliography without opening each source and confirming that it exists and supports the stated claim.\n\n**Teaching prompts** should identify the subject, learner age or level, curriculum outcome, prior knowledge, available resources and assessment method. Request likely misconceptions and differentiated activities, then check accuracy, inclusivity and suitability before classroom use.\n\n**Data-analysis prompts** should describe the dataset’s fields and the question to investigate. Ask the model to inspect missing values, duplicates, data types and outliers before calculating. Require formulas, assumptions and reproducible steps. Do not upload identifiable or confidential data to an unapproved service.\n\n**Creative prompts** can specify audience, purpose, tone, format, visual direction and brand constraints. For image generation, describe the subject, composition, viewpoint, lighting and exclusions. Verify technical diagrams, logos, text and factual details manually.\n\n**Coding prompts** should include language, runtime, framework, expected behaviour, input/output examples, constraints and tests. Ask for explanation and edge cases. Review generated code for security, dependencies, permissions and compatibility before execution.\n\n**Practical lab:** Use one topic to produce a research question, lesson outline, data-analysis plan and creative brief. Compare the information each prompt must provide and identify the verification method for each output.\n\n**Checkpoint:** Explain why the same generic prompt is unlikely to produce equally useful outputs for a research review and a classroom worksheet.","# Module 5: Prompt Evaluation, Iteration and Safety\n\nPrompt quality should be evaluated against the task, not by whether the response sounds impressive. Create a rubric with criteria such as correctness, relevance, completeness, clarity, format compliance, source quality, safety and usefulness. Define what a successful result looks like before testing.\n\nRun repeatable tests using representative inputs. Include normal cases, missing information, contradictory instructions and edge cases. If a model is stochastic, outputs may vary between runs. Record the model or tool version, date, prompt version, test input and observed result when reproducibility matters.\n\nCommon failure patterns include hallucinated facts, omitted constraints, overconfident language, biased framing, incorrect calculations, fabricated citations and leakage of sensitive information. Improve prompts by narrowing scope, supplying trusted source material, requesting uncertainty statements, asking for intermediate checks or adding a human approval stage. Prompt changes cannot solve every model limitation.\n\nTreat external documents and retrieved web content as untrusted. Do not allow an instruction hidden in a document to override system policy or reveal confidential information. In connected workflows, limit tool permissions and require confirmation before sending messages, changing records, spending money or publishing content.\n\nPrivacy and safety begin before prompting. Classify data, use approved systems, minimise inputs and remove identifiers where possible. Respect copyright, academic integrity and applicable organisational policy.\n\n**Exercise:** Build a ten-case prompt test set. Score outputs with a five-criterion rubric, identify the most frequent failure and revise the prompt. Report both improvements and remaining limitations.\n\n**Review question:** Why should a prompt evaluation include edge cases rather than only the easiest example?","# Module 6: Reusable Prompt Systems — Capstone and Assessment\n\nA prompt library is a managed collection of templates for recurring tasks. Each entry should include a title, purpose, intended user, required inputs, template, example, quality criteria, known limitations, privacy classification, owner and revision date. Remove outdated prompts and record significant changes.\n\nA prompt workflow may contain several stages: intake and validation; planning; generation; checking against source material; human approval; and final output. Each stage should have a defined input and output. Do not let an unverified generated answer become trusted source data for later stages without review.\n\n**Capstone:** Build a reusable prompt pack for one real but low-risk workflow. Include three prompts, placeholders, usage instructions, a sample input, sample output, a test set of at least five cases, an evaluation rubric, a privacy note and a human review checklist. Demonstrate one iteration based on observed failure.\n\n**Assessment — 20 marks:**\n1. Identify five parts of a well-structured prompt (4).\n2. Explain the value and limitation of role instructions (2).\n3. Describe few-shot prompting and one suitable use (3).\n4. Design a prompt for a research or teaching task with clear constraints (4).\n5. Give three criteria for evaluating model output (3).\n6. Explain two safety controls for a tool-connected AI workflow (4).\n\n**Marking guidance:** Credit prompts that are specific, testable and suitable for their audience. Full marks require verification and safety steps, not merely elaborate wording.\n\n**Professional practice:** Keep prompts short enough to maintain clarity, test them against real task variations and update them when the tool, policy or workflow changes. A reusable prompt is a starting point for controlled work, not a substitute for expertise."];
notes["ai-for-teachers"]=["# Module 1: AI Literacy for Educators\n\nAI literacy for educators includes understanding what AI systems do, where their outputs come from, what they cannot reliably do and how their use affects teaching and learning. Teachers need practical knowledge rather than exaggerated claims that AI will either solve every classroom problem or make teachers unnecessary.\n\nGenerative AI can draft explanations, questions, lesson outlines, translations and feedback suggestions. Other systems may transcribe speech, support accessibility, analyse patterns or recommend resources. Distinguish these capabilities from ordinary digital tools and from automated decisions made using fixed rules.\n\nA language model predicts text based on learned patterns and the context supplied. It may produce fluent but incorrect explanations, fabricated references or examples that do not match the curriculum. It may also reflect cultural or language bias. Teachers should check subject accuracy, local relevance, reading level and inclusion.\n\nUse AI to support professional work: brainstorm alternatives, generate a first draft, ask for misconceptions, simplify a passage or create practice questions. Retain responsibility for learning outcomes, assessment decisions, safeguarding and relationships with learners. Never treat an AI-generated learner profile as a diagnosis.\n\n**Classroom scenario:** A teacher asks an AI assistant to explain chemical bonding. The response incorrectly states that ionic compounds consist of discrete molecules. Identify the scientific error, correct the explanation and describe the verification source you would consult.\n\n**Practical task:** Create a personal AI literacy checklist covering capability, limitations, accuracy, bias, privacy, accessibility and human accountability. Apply it to one tool before classroom use.","# Module 2: AI for Lesson Planning and Curriculum Alignment\n\nEffective lesson planning begins with the intended learning, not with the tool. Identify curriculum standards, measurable outcomes, prior knowledge, key vocabulary, likely misconceptions, available resources, time and assessment evidence. AI can help generate options, but the teacher checks alignment with the official curriculum and the learners’ context.\n\nWrite outcomes using observable verbs such as identify, explain, calculate, compare, interpret, design or justify. “Understand acids” is difficult to assess; “classify common substances using indicator results and explain the colour change” is more observable.\n\nPrompt example: “For [grade/level] Chemistry, design a 40-minute lesson on [topic]. Align it to this supplied curriculum outcome: [text]. Include three measurable objectives, prior knowledge, a safe demonstration using available equipment, a learner activity, likely misconceptions, formative questions and an exit ticket. Do not invent curriculum codes or unsafe procedures.”\n\nReview generated plans for sequencing, realistic timing, equipment availability, safety, language level and cultural relevance. A practical activity must include risk assessment and supervision appropriate to the school. AI should not replace the institution’s laboratory safety procedures.\n\nDifferentiation may include vocabulary support, worked examples, diagrams, scaffolded questions, extension problems and alternative ways to demonstrate learning. Avoid labelling learners permanently by ability. Use evidence of current understanding and allow learners to progress.\n\n**Practical lab:** Prepare a lesson plan for a topic you teach. Compare the AI draft with the official syllabus, edit the learning outcomes and annotate at least five changes with reasons.\n\n**Checkpoint:** Explain why a polished lesson plan can still be unsuitable if it does not match the curriculum or available resources.","# Module 3: AI for Assessment, Feedback and Academic Integrity\n\nAssessment should measure the intended learning outcome. AI can help draft multiple-choice items, short-answer questions, rubrics and feedback prompts. Teachers must check that each item is accurate, unambiguous, appropriately difficult and aligned to the taught content.\n\nGood multiple-choice questions have one defensible best answer and plausible distractors based on common misconceptions. Avoid grammatical clues, overlapping options and trick wording. Check calculations independently and ensure diagrams or data tables contain all necessary information.\n\nA rubric describes criteria and performance levels. Criteria should be observable, such as scientific accuracy, method, evidence, reasoning and communication. Avoid vague labels such as “excellent effort” unless the behaviour is defined. Moderate assessments with colleagues where appropriate.\n\nAI-assisted feedback should be specific, actionable and respectful. A useful structure is: what is correct, what needs improvement, why it matters and the next step. Do not allow automated feedback to make high-stakes decisions without teacher review. Learners should have a way to ask for clarification or challenge an error.\n\nAcademic integrity policies should explain permitted support, prohibited assistance, attribution and how learners demonstrate their own understanding. Do not rely on AI-detection scores as definitive proof of misconduct; such systems can be inaccurate and require careful, fair investigation under school policy.\n\n**Practical task:** Draft five questions and a marking guide for one lesson. Solve every question yourself, check the answer key and revise ambiguous wording before use.","# Module 4: Differentiation, Inclusion and Accessibility\n\nAI may help teachers create alternative explanations, vocabulary lists, reading-level adjustments, translation drafts, caption drafts and varied practice. These supports can widen access, but they must be checked with learners and appropriate specialists. A generated adaptation may unintentionally remove important subject meaning or introduce errors.\n\nUniversal Design for Learning encourages multiple means of engagement, representation and action or expression. A science topic might use a concise explanation, labelled diagram, demonstration, vocabulary list and oral explanation. Offer meaningful choices while keeping the learning objective consistent.\n\nAccessibility checks include readable fonts, sufficient colour contrast, meaningful headings, captions, alternative text for informative images, keyboard access and clear language. Do not communicate meaning through colour alone. Verify translations, especially technical vocabulary and safety instructions.\n\nBe careful with sensitive information about disability, health, behaviour or family circumstances. Use only approved systems and share the minimum necessary data. Do not ask an AI system to diagnose a learner or decide eligibility for support.\n\n**Case study:** A learner has difficulty reading a dense worksheet. Design three ways to make the content more accessible without lowering the scientific objective. Explain how you would check that the adaptations preserve meaning.\n\n**Practical lab:** Take a short, non-sensitive teaching passage and create a glossary, a simplified explanation and a diagram brief. Compare all versions with the original for accuracy and accessibility.","# Module 5: Classroom Practice, Professional Learning and Safeguarding\n\nClassroom AI use should have a clear learning purpose. Learners might compare an AI explanation with a textbook, critique a generated argument, identify errors in a calculation or use a tutor-style prompt for guided practice. The activity should make thinking visible rather than encourage copying.\n\nTeach learners to ask: What evidence supports this claim? Is the source real? What might be missing? Does the answer fit the question? Can I reproduce the calculation? Is the content fair and safe to share? Model how to correct an AI response and acknowledge assistance according to school rules.\n\nSafeguarding is essential. Do not direct learners to disclose personal problems to an unmonitored chatbot. AI tools are not substitutes for trained counsellors, child-protection staff or emergency services. Follow school procedures for concerns and use approved age-appropriate platforms.\n\nProfessional learning should be collaborative. Teachers can share reviewed prompts, lesson examples, errors discovered and effective safeguards. Keep a record of tool versions and policy changes. Evaluate whether the tool improves learning, saves meaningful time or creates additional checking work.\n\n**Implementation exercise:** Plan a 20-minute classroom activity in which learners critically evaluate an AI-generated explanation. Include learning outcomes, source comparison, group roles, assessment evidence and a clear rule against entering personal data.\n\n**Reflection:** What would you do if a learner submitted AI-generated work as their own? Explain a fair response based on teaching, evidence and the school’s academic-integrity policy.","# Module 6: Teacher Capstone and Assessment\n\n**Capstone — Design an AI-supported teaching sequence:** Select a syllabus topic and prepare a complete teaching pack: curriculum outcome, measurable objectives, lesson sequence, teacher-reviewed explanation, differentiated activity, assessment questions, marking guide, learner guidance on AI use, accessibility review, privacy safeguards and reflection. Include the original AI prompt and document corrections made during review.\n\n**Evaluation plan:** Identify a baseline, such as completion time, learner performance on a concept check or quality of feedback. Collect only appropriate information. Compare outcomes fairly and consider alternative explanations. Do not claim that AI caused an improvement based on a single observation.\n\n**Assessment — 20 marks:**\n1. Explain three AI capabilities and two limitations relevant to teachers (4).\n2. Write a curriculum-aligned lesson-planning prompt (3).\n3. Identify four checks for AI-generated assessment questions (4).\n4. Describe two ways to support accessibility without changing the learning objective (3).\n5. Explain two safeguarding or privacy rules for classroom use (3).\n6. Propose a measurable way to evaluate an AI-supported lesson (3).\n\n**Marking guidance:** Reward curriculum alignment, accurate subject knowledge, practical inclusion and clear safeguards. Answers should show that the teacher retains responsibility for assessment and learner welfare.\n\n**Professional reflection:** Identify one low-risk AI use you will pilot, the approval or policy check required, the evidence you will collect and the conditions under which you would stop using the tool."];
notes["ai-productivity-business"]=["# Module 1: AI for Personal Productivity and Work Planning\n\nProductivity is not simply completing more tasks; it is directing effort toward useful outcomes while maintaining quality. AI can help turn unstructured notes into plans, draft routine messages, summarise non-sensitive documents and organise ideas. It can also create extra work when outputs require extensive correction.\n\nStart with a task inventory. Record task frequency, time required, repetition, error consequences, data sensitivity and the amount of human judgement needed. Tasks that are repetitive, low-risk and easy to verify are stronger candidates for assistance than confidential or high-impact decisions.\n\nA practical planning prompt might be: “Organise these non-confidential tasks into urgent, important and later categories. Preserve every deadline exactly. Identify missing owners or dates as ‘not stated’. Suggest a realistic daily plan with breaks.” Verify the list against the original notes.\n\nMeeting support can include agenda drafts, action-item extraction and summary formatting. Check names, decisions, owners and deadlines. A transcript may omit context or misrecognise speakers, so participants should confirm important minutes before they become official records.\n\nBuild a repeatable workflow: capture information, classify it, draft with AI if appropriate, verify against the source, obtain approval and store the final version in the approved system. Avoid copying confidential business or employee information into unapproved tools.\n\n**Practical task:** Choose one repetitive low-risk task. Measure the current time and error rate, test an AI-assisted method with fictional or approved data and record time saved plus checking time. Decide whether the workflow genuinely improves productivity.","# Module 2: AI for Writing, Editing and Content Production\n\nAI can assist with outlining, drafting, rewriting, proofreading, summarising and adapting content for different audiences. The writer must supply accurate facts, purpose, audience, tone and source material. Generated text should be treated as a draft rather than an automatically publishable document.\n\nA reliable writing workflow is: define the reader and objective; collect verified facts; create an outline; draft; check claims and quotations; edit for clarity and voice; check accessibility and rights; approve; then publish. Keep a record of sources and distinguish direct quotations from paraphrases.\n\nPrompt example: “Draft a 500-word guide for first-time customers explaining [service]. Use only the verified facts below. Use plain English, clear headings and a helpful tone. Do not invent prices, guarantees or contact details. Add a list of claims that need confirmation.”\n\nFor editing, specify whether the model should correct grammar, improve flow, shorten text or change tone. Ask it to preserve names, numbers and technical terms. Compare revisions with the source to ensure meaning has not changed. Do not let a rewrite silently remove important qualifications.\n\nRepurposing content means adapting one verified idea for different formats, such as an article, email, social post and short script. Each platform has its own audience and conventions. Avoid repetitive low-value publishing; add useful examples and original insight.\n\n**Practical lab:** Produce one short article and two adaptations from a verified source brief. Highlight which facts remain identical and what changes for each audience. Proofread every version before publication.","# Module 3: AI for Marketing, Customers and Search Visibility\n\nMarketing begins with a customer problem and a clear value proposition. AI can support audience research, idea generation, campaign drafts, keyword clustering, message variants and reporting. It cannot replace real customer evidence or justify misleading claims.\n\nDefine the audience by needs and context rather than stereotypes. A useful customer profile describes the problem, desired outcome, barriers, decision factors and information sources. Validate assumptions through interviews, surveys, analytics or actual sales data.\n\nA campaign brief should state objective, audience, offer, message, channel, budget, period, call to action and success measures. Example measures include qualified enquiries, conversion rate, cost per acquisition and repeat purchase. Define each metric and its data source before launch.\n\nFor search content, answer a genuine question clearly, use descriptive headings, include original examples and cite reliable sources. AI-generated keyword lists do not prove search demand. Avoid keyword stuffing, doorway pages, copied content and mass-produced pages that offer no distinct value. Search engines and users reward usefulness, not the mere presence of AI.\n\nCustomer-service drafts must be checked against actual policy and account records. AI should not invent delivery dates, refunds, prices or promises. Escalate complaints, sensitive matters and uncertain cases to an authorised person.\n\n**Practical task:** Build a campaign brief for a real or fictional service. Separate verified facts from assumptions, propose two message variants and define one primary KPI plus two quality safeguards.","# Module 4: AI for Small-Business Operations and Decision Support\n\nSmall businesses can use AI to organise product descriptions, draft standard operating procedures, summarise non-confidential feedback, brainstorm service improvements and prepare first drafts of business plans. The owner remains responsible for pricing, legal compliance, finances, customer promises and operational decisions.\n\nMap a business process from trigger to outcome. For example, an enquiry workflow may include receiving a message, identifying the request, checking required information, drafting a response, human approval, sending and recording the result. Define exception routes for complaints, refunds, suspected fraud and requests involving personal data.\n\nAI can help structure a business model canvas: customer segments, value proposition, channels, customer relationships, revenue streams, key resources, activities, partners and costs. Treat suggested entries as hypotheses to test. Interview potential customers and calculate realistic costs before investing.\n\nFor financial planning, verify all figures independently. A cash-flow forecast should show opening balance, expected receipts, payments and closing balance for each period. AI may help explain a spreadsheet formula, but it should not invent sales, tax obligations or funding terms.\n\nVendor selection should consider data protection, security, reliability, cost, support, export capability, lock-in and terms of service. Keep backups and ensure the business can continue essential work if a tool becomes unavailable.\n\n**Exercise:** Choose one business workflow and create a process map, identify two safe AI support points, one decision that must remain human-controlled and a fallback method.","# Module 5: Automation, Measurement and Responsible Use\n\nAutomation links steps across applications, such as a form submission, spreadsheet update, notification and task assignment. Before automating, define the trigger, input validation, permissions, transformation, approval, output, error handling and audit record. Test with fictional data and unusual cases.\n\nA workflow should fail safely. What happens if a required field is missing, a service is unavailable, a duplicate request arrives or an incorrect record is detected? Use clear error messages, retry rules and a manual route. Do not allow repeated retries to create duplicate payments, messages or records.\n\nMeasure performance against a baseline. Useful measures include time per task, error rate, response time, customer satisfaction and cost. Include the time spent reviewing AI outputs. A faster process that increases errors or harms customer trust is not an improvement.\n\nResponsible use includes privacy, security, fairness, copyright, transparency and human accountability. Collect only necessary information, restrict access and disclose AI use where law, policy or customer expectations require it. Review generated marketing claims and ensure they are truthful and substantiated.\n\n**Practical lab:** Design a low-risk automation for sorting non-sensitive enquiries. Write test cases for a normal enquiry, incomplete message, duplicate, complaint and unsupported request. Specify when the system must stop and send the case to a person.\n\n**Review:** Explain why a human approval checkpoint is important before an automated system sends a consequential message or changes a customer record.","# Module 6: Business Productivity Capstone and Assessment\n\n**Capstone — Improve one business workflow:** Select a repetitive process and document its current steps, users, data, time, error points and customer impact. Propose an AI-assisted or automated redesign. Include a prompt or workflow specification, data classification, permissions, human approval, exception handling, fallback plan, test cases, baseline and success measures.\n\nRun a small pilot only with authorised tools and suitable data. Compare results with the baseline and report limitations. Do not claim success based solely on the tool’s own generated summary; use measured evidence.\n\n**Assessment — 20 marks:**\n1. Identify four tasks that may be suitable for AI assistance and explain why (4).\n2. Write a content-generation prompt with source and accuracy constraints (3).\n3. Define a campaign objective and two measurable KPIs (3).\n4. Explain two risks of AI in small-business operations (3).\n5. Describe four components of a safe automation workflow (4).\n6. Explain how to evaluate whether a productivity change is worthwhile (3).\n\n**Marking guidance:** Credit realistic workflow design, measurable indicators, accurate distinction between assumptions and facts, and appropriate human controls.\n\n**Reflection:** Which task should remain fully human-led? Which could be assisted? What evidence, approval and safeguards are needed before any automation is used with customers or business records?"];
notes["responsible-ai"]=["# Module 1: Responsible AI Foundations — Accountability and Human Judgment\n\nResponsible AI is the design, development, procurement and use of AI in ways that respect people, rights, safety and legitimate purposes. It is not a single technical feature. Responsibility spans the organisations that build systems, the organisations that deploy them and the people who use their outputs.\n\nCore principles commonly include human agency and oversight, technical robustness and safety, privacy and data governance, transparency, diversity and fairness, social and environmental well-being, and accountability. The exact legal requirements differ by jurisdiction and application, so consult current local law and organisational policy.\n\nA useful risk assessment begins with purpose: What problem is being addressed? Who is affected? What decisions may follow? What data is needed? What happens if the system is wrong? Is AI necessary, or would a simpler process be safer? High-impact uses require stronger assessment, documentation, monitoring and routes for human intervention.\n\nHuman oversight must be meaningful. Reviewers need relevant competence, access to supporting evidence, authority to reject a recommendation and enough time to assess it. A rubber-stamp approval does not provide meaningful oversight. People affected by a decision should be able to seek an explanation or correction where appropriate.\n\n**Case study:** A school considers using AI to recommend which learners receive additional support. Identify the intended benefit, affected groups, risks of inaccurate predictions, data required, human decision-maker and appeal route. Explain why a score should not determine a learner’s future automatically.\n\n**Practical task:** Write a responsible-use statement for one AI application. Include purpose, benefits, limits, accountable owner and conditions under which use should stop.","# Module 2: Bias, Fairness and Inclusion\n\nBias is a systematic pattern that can produce unfair or unequal outcomes. It may arise from under-represented training data, inaccurate labels, historical discrimination, unsuitable measurement, model design or the context in which a system is deployed. Removing a sensitive field does not necessarily remove bias because other variables may act as proxies.\n\nFairness is context-dependent. A recruitment tool, translation system and educational support model affect people in different ways. First define the outcome that matters, identify affected groups and consider relevant legal and ethical obligations. Do not assume one statistical fairness measure is appropriate for every case.\n\nEvaluation should use representative data and examine error rates across relevant groups where lawful and ethically justified. Small sample sizes create uncertainty. Results should be interpreted carefully and supplemented by qualitative review. Ask who is missing from the data and whether people can challenge inaccurate records.\n\nInclusive design considers language, disability, connectivity, affordability, age and cultural context. Provide accessible alternatives and do not make an essential service available only through an AI interface. Test with the people who will use or be affected by the system.\n\n**Example:** A speech-recognition tool performs less accurately for some accents. A fair response includes testing across accents, offering correction options, informing users of limitations and avoiding high-stakes decisions based solely on transcription.\n\n**Exercise:** Choose an AI application and create a fairness review with: affected groups, possible sources of bias, evidence to collect, mitigation, human review and a way to report harm.","# Module 3: Personal Information, Privacy and Data Governance\n\nPersonal data is information relating to an identified or identifiable person. Sensitive categories and legal definitions vary by jurisdiction. Names, contact details, learner records, employee information, health details and combinations of indirect identifiers may create privacy risks.\n\nData minimisation means collecting and sharing only what is necessary for a defined purpose. Before entering information into an AI tool, ask whether the task can be completed using fictional, anonymised or aggregated data. Removing names alone may not prevent re-identification when dates, locations or rare characteristics remain.\n\nCheck the tool’s privacy notice, data retention, training use, access controls, data location, subprocessors, deletion process and contractual terms. Use institutionally approved systems for work records. Do not assume that a public chatbot is appropriate for confidential information.\n\nData governance assigns responsibilities for collection, access, accuracy, correction, retention, deletion and incident response. Use least-privilege permissions, secure storage, strong authentication and audit logs. Keep data only as long as necessary and follow applicable retention rules.\n\n**Practical scenario:** A teacher wants AI to summarise learner performance. Design a safer method using aggregated fictional or authorised data. Identify which fields are essential, which should be removed, who may access the output and how the teacher will verify conclusions.\n\n**Checklist:** Purpose, lawful basis where applicable, necessity, data minimisation, approved tool, access, retention, security, transparency and correction route.","# Module 4: Misinformation, Deepfakes and Verification\n\nGenerative AI can create convincing text, images, audio and video. A deepfake may imitate a person’s appearance or voice. Synthetic content can be used for entertainment and legitimate creative work, but it can also enable fraud, harassment, impersonation and misinformation.\n\nDo not rely on visual appearance or an AI detector alone to establish authenticity. Detection tools can produce false positives and false negatives, especially as generation methods change. Verify the original source, publication context, date, corroborating reports and provenance where available.\n\nFor a suspicious request involving money or confidential information, verify through a known independent channel. Do not use contact details supplied only in the suspicious message. A voice that sounds familiar is not sufficient proof of identity. Follow organisational payment and approval procedures.\n\nWhen evaluating a claim, separate the claim from the evidence. Identify the original publisher, author, date, primary document and independent corroboration. Reverse-image search or metadata may provide clues, but neither is conclusive in every case. Preserve evidence if an incident must be reported.\n\n**Classroom activity:** Compare a fictional viral claim with a primary source and two independent credible sources. Record what is confirmed, contradicted and still unknown. Explain why sharing an unverified claim can cause harm.\n\n**Practical checklist:** Pause before sharing; locate the original; check date and context; corroborate independently; contact the person or organisation through a trusted channel; report suspected fraud or abuse.","# Module 5: Digital Safety, Security and Ethical Practice\n\nResponsible AI use depends on ordinary digital security. Use unique passwords, multi-factor authentication, software updates, secure devices and approved storage. Be alert to phishing messages that use polished language, personalised details or generated voices. Verify unexpected links and requests through trusted channels.\n\nAI systems can introduce specific risks: prompt injection, data leakage, excessive tool permissions, insecure integrations and over-reliance on generated outputs. Treat documents and retrieved content as untrusted data. Limit what an AI assistant can access and require approval before it sends messages, changes records, publishes content or makes purchases.\n\nEthical use also includes honesty, attribution, respect for copyright and avoiding impersonation. Do not fabricate testimonials, credentials, references or evidence. Follow academic-integrity rules and disclose AI assistance when required. Check the licences and terms governing images, text, code and other generated or supplied material.\n\nCreate an incident response route. Users should know how to report exposed data, harmful output, impersonation, suspected fraud or a system error. Preserve relevant evidence, notify the responsible team and follow approved containment and reporting procedures. Do not investigate beyond your authority.\n\n**Exercise:** Draft a one-page safe-use checklist for a workplace or school. Include account security, data handling, source verification, permitted uses, human approval and incident reporting.\n\n**Reflection:** Identify one situation where the safest action is not to use AI at all, and explain the alternative.","# Module 6: Responsible AI Capstone and Assessment\n\n**Capstone — Responsible AI impact review:** Select an AI use case and prepare a review covering purpose and necessity, stakeholders, potential benefits, affected groups, data flows, privacy, security, fairness, accuracy, transparency, human oversight, appeal and correction, monitoring and exit conditions. Distinguish evidence from assumptions and identify which legal or institutional requirements must be checked locally.\n\nCreate a risk register with each risk, likelihood, impact, existing controls, additional treatment, accountable owner and review date. Prioritise risks according to consequences rather than simply listing them. Include a plan to monitor incidents and reconsider the system when conditions change.\n\n**Assessment — 20 marks:**\n1. Explain four principles of responsible AI (4).\n2. Identify three possible sources of algorithmic bias (3).\n3. Describe four privacy and data-minimisation controls (4).\n4. Explain why an AI detector cannot conclusively authenticate a deepfake (3).\n5. Identify three security risks in connected AI workflows (3).\n6. Explain meaningful human oversight and accountability (3).\n\n**Marking guidance:** Full-credit answers connect each principle to a practical action, responsible owner and evidence of implementation. Accept locally relevant laws and frameworks when accurately named and applied.\n\n**Final reflection:** Who could be harmed by this AI use? Can they understand and challenge its effects? What evidence would show that the benefits outweigh the risks? Who has authority to pause or stop the system? If these questions cannot be answered, the use case is not ready for deployment."];
E.makeLongNotes=function(slug,course,module,level){const items=notes[slug];const index=course&&Array.isArray(course.modules)?course.modules.indexOf(module):-1;if(items&&index>=0&&items[index])return items[index];return typeof previous==="function"?previous.call(E,slug,course,module,level):"";};
})();